## Proof

Let $a=BC$, $b=CA$, $c=AB$, and let $s=\tfrac{a+b+c}{2}$. Place $A$ at the origin and let $\mathbf b,\mathbf c$ be the position vectors of $B,C$, so $|\mathbf b|=c$, $|\mathbf c|=b$, $\mathbf b\!\cdot\!\mathbf c=bc\cos A$.

Let $P$ vary on $BC$ with $BP=t$ (so $CP=a-t$). Since $D\in AB$ with $BD=t$,
$$D=\Big(1-\tfrac{t}{c}\Big)\mathbf b=\tfrac{c-t}{c}\,\mathbf b.$$
Since $E\in AC$ with $CE=a-t$,
$$E=\Big(1-\tfrac{a-t}{b}\Big)\mathbf c=\tfrac{b-a+t}{b}\,\mathbf c.$$

**Circle equation.** The circumcircle of $ADE$ passes through $A=\mathbf 0$, so it has the form
$$|\mathbf x|^2+\mathbf U\cdot\mathbf x=0$$
for some vector $\mathbf U$ (depending on $t$). Substituting $D$ and $E$:
$$\mathbf U\cdot\mathbf b=-c(c-t)=-c^2+ct,\qquad \mathbf U\cdot\mathbf c=-b(b-a+t)=-b^2+ab-bt.$$
Write $\mathbf U=\mathbf U_0+t\,\mathbf U_1$. Comparing coefficients of $t$:
$$\mathbf U_1\!\cdot\!\mathbf b=c,\quad \mathbf U_1\!\cdot\!\mathbf c=-b;\qquad \mathbf U_0\!\cdot\!\mathbf b=-c^2,\quad \mathbf U_0\!\cdot\!\mathbf c=b(a-b).$$

**Fixed-point condition.** A point $Q=\mathbf q\ne A$ lies on every such circle iff
$$|\mathbf q|^2+\mathbf U\cdot\mathbf q=0\quad\text{for all }t,$$
i.e. $\mathbf U_1\!\cdot\!\mathbf q=0$ and $|\mathbf q|^2+\mathbf U_0\!\cdot\!\mathbf q=0$.

**Finding $\mathbf U_1$.** Writing $\mathbf U_1=\mu\mathbf b+\nu\mathbf c$ and solving
$$\mu c^2+\nu(\mathbf b\!\cdot\!\mathbf c)=c,\qquad \mu(\mathbf b\!\cdot\!\mathbf c)+\nu b^2=-b$$
gives (using $\mathbf b\!\cdot\!\mathbf c=bc\cos A$ and $c^2b^2-(\mathbf b\!\cdot\!\mathbf c)^2=b^2c^2\sin^2 A$)
$$\mathbf U_1=\frac{1+\cos A}{\sin^2 A}\Big(\frac{\mathbf b}{c}-\frac{\mathbf c}{b}\Big)=\frac{1+\cos A}{\sin^2 A}\,(\hat e_{AB}-\hat e_{AC}).$$
Hence $\mathbf U_1\!\cdot\!\mathbf q=0$ iff $\mathbf q\perp(\hat e_{AB}-\hat e_{AC})$. Since
$$(\hat e_{AB}+\hat e_{AC})\cdot(\hat e_{AB}-\hat e_{AC})=|\hat e_{AB}|^2-|\hat e_{AC}|^2=0,$$
the locus is the line through $A$ in direction $\hat e_{AB}+\hat e_{AC}$: **the internal angle bisector of $\angle A$.**

**Locating $Q$ on the bisector.** Write $\mathbf q=\lambda\big(\tfrac{\mathbf b}{c}+\tfrac{\mathbf c}{b}\big)$. Then
$$|\mathbf q|^2=2\lambda^2(1+\cos A),\qquad \mathbf U_0\!\cdot\!\mathbf q=\lambda\Big(\frac{-c^2}{c}+\frac{b(a-b)}{b}\Big)=\lambda(a-b-c).$$
The equation $|\mathbf q|^2+\mathbf U_0\!\cdot\!\mathbf q=0$ yields
$$\lambda\big(2\lambda(1+\cos A)-(b+c-a)\big)=0.$$
The nonzero solution is $\lambda=\dfrac{b+c-a}{2(1+\cos A)}=\dfrac{s-a}{2\cos^2(A/2)}$ (using $1+\cos A=2\cos^2\!\tfrac A2$ and $b+c-a=2(s-a)$).

**Distance $AQ$.** Since $\big|\tfrac{\mathbf b}{c}+\tfrac{\mathbf c}{b}\big|^2=2+2\cos A=4\cos^2\!\tfrac A2$,
$$AQ=|\mathbf q|=\lambda\cdot 2\cos\tfrac A2=\frac{s-a}{\cos(A/2)}.$$

**Identifying $Q$ as the incenter.** Let $I$ be the incenter and $T$ the tangency point of the incircle with $AB$. Then $AT=s-a$ (tangent length from $A$), $IT\perp AB$, and $\angle IAT=A/2$ (since $AI$ is the angle bisector). From right triangle $AIT$,
$$AI=\frac{AT}{\cos(A/2)}=\frac{s-a}{\cos(A/2)}=AQ.$$
Both $I$ and $Q$ lie on the internal angle bisector from $A$ at the same distance from $A$, so $Q=I$.

Therefore, as $P$ traces $BC$, the circumcircle of $ADE$ always passes through the incenter $I$ of $\triangle ABC$ — a fixed point. $\blacksquare$

